    "summary": "High‑energy Playing In The Band and a legendary Phil solo",
    "blurb": "The February ’73 Iowa City show shines with a blistering Playing In The Band, a seamless Truckin' → Nobody's Fault But Mine → Eyes Of The World → Sugar Magnolia flow, and an unforgettable Phil Lesh bass solo. Jerry’s lead tones are crisp and adventurous, while the rhythm section locks tight throughout.",
    "review": "Feb 24 1973 at the University of Iowa finds the Dead firing on all cylinders. The first set builds nicely through Bertha and Mexicali Blues before exploding into a scorching Playing In The Band – Garcia’s guitar glides with perfect tone, venturing into uncharted territory while Phil’s Alembic‑tone bass roars in distinct stereo separation. Box of Rain and Tennessee Jed add the usual warmth, then the second set turns up the voltage: a soulful Wave That Flag leads straight into a monster Truckin' sequence that seamlessly morphs into Nobody's Fault But Mine, rolls into an expansive Eyes Of The World, and lands on a jubilant Sugar Magnolia. The highlight is Phil’s legendary 1973 solo captured in crystal‑clear soundboard detail – crunchy low end, sparkling highs, and the Wall‑of‑Sound effect that makes this fragment a must‑listen. Although only a nine‑minute snippet of the solo survives, it epitomizes why early ’73 remains a high point for bass‑centric improvisation.",
